---
import DocPage from '../../components/DocPage.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
const source = 'https://github.com/gua-project/gua/blob/gua-v1.1.0/';
---
<DocPage eyebrow="DOCS / PACKAGES &amp; FEATURES" title="Choose Gua packages and features (1.1.0)" lead="Start with the engine host and test API you need, then add semantic baselines, visual comparison, recording, or AI tools to that setup." description="Choose and use Gua 1.1.0 packages for UI tests, additional observations, Trace, semantic snapshots, spatial queries, and game input." section="docs">
  <h2 id="packages">Choose packages for your workflow</h2>
  <p>For .NET UI tests, use <code>Gua.Testing</code> with an engine host. Add optional packages only for the comparisons or replay your tests need. Keep the Gua packages in one project on the same version.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>What you want to do</th><th>Package or integration</th><th>Start here</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Run Godot UI tests</td><td><code>Gua.Testing.Godot</code> + Godot GDScript addon; the test package brings in <code>Gua.Testing</code>/<code>Gua.Core</code>.</td><td><a href="/godot/testing/">Godot testing</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Run Unity UI tests</td><td><code>Gua.Testing.Unity</code> + <code>com.link1345.gua</code> UPM integration; the test package brings in <code>Gua.Testing</code>/<code>Gua.Core</code>.</td><td><a href="/unity/testing/">Unity testing</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Compare semantic UI/World state</td><td><code>Gua.Testing.Snapshots</code></td><td><a href="/docs/dotnet-packages/#semantic-snapshots">Semantic baselines</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Compare rendered PNGs</td><td><code>Gua.Testing.Visual</code></td><td><a href="/visual-testing/">Visual testing</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Record and replay test journeys</td><td><code>Gua.Testing.Recording</code></td><td><a href="/recording/">Recording</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Implement a managed game-side adapter</td><td><code>Gua.Runtime</code> + <code>Gua.Core</code></td><td><a href="/docs/gua-runtime/">Runtime implementation</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Connect AI tools to a desktop game</td><td><code>gui-mcp</code> npm package</td><td><a href="/mcp/">Native MCP</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Expose a browser game's tools in its page</td><td><code>gua-webmcp</code> npm package</td><td><a href="/webmcp/">Browser WebMCP</a></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Use shared types in TypeScript</td><td><code>gua-value-tools</code> for Value validation/equality; <code>gua-world-tools</code> for World types/selectors.</td><td><a href="/docs/value/">Value</a> · <a href="/world-object-tree/">World objects</a></td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <CodeBlock title="Example: Godot tests with semantic baselines" lang="powershell" code={'dotnet add package Gua.Testing.Godot --version 1.1.0\ndotnet add package Gua.Testing.Snapshots --version 1.1.0'} />
  <p>The game also needs its engine integration; installing a test package does not install the Godot addon or Unity UPM package. See <a href="#distribution">distribution</a> before choosing an engine archive.</p>
  <h2 id="snapshots">Compare semantic state with Gua.Testing.Snapshots</h2>
  <p>Use <code>GuaSemanticSnapshots.ExpectSnapshot</code> for UI and optional World JSON baselines. It compares IDs, hierarchy, text, values, state, and publication order while excluding geometry and runtime metadata by default. Missing or changed baselines fail; update them explicitly and review the diff. Configure mask/ignore rules before capture. For clipping or other pixel changes, add <code>Gua.Testing.Visual</code> instead. See the <a href="/docs/dotnet-packages/#semantic-snapshots">installation and comparison example</a> and <a href={source + 'bindings/dotnet/src/Gua.Testing.Snapshots/README.md'}>Snapshots API</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="observe">Expose additional values with Gua.Runtime</h2>
  <p>Use <code>Gua.Runtime</code> to register additional typed observations on a published UI/Object owner. The built-in Godot addon and Unity UPM integration expose the same registration model. Keep the owner and registration token alive with the object; disposal or tree removal ends the registration. Values use <code>Gua.Core.GuaValue</code> and <code>GuaEnumCatalog</code>; TypeScript consumers use <a href="/docs/value/"><code>gua-value-tools</code></a>.</p>
  <p>External .NET tests use <code>Gua.Testing.GuaWebSocketContext</code> to read and subscribe. <code>gui-mcp</code>, <code>gua-webmcp</code>, and Inspector expose <code>get_observe_snapshot</code>, <code>subscribe_observations</code>, <code>poll_observations</code>, and <code>unsubscribe_observations</code> when the host advertises <code>observe_v1</code>. Player visibility remains host-controlled. Reconnect or a notification gap requires a fresh subscription; it is not an empty successful result. See <a href={source + 'bindings/dotnet/src/Gua.Runtime/GuaRuntime.Observe.cs'}>managed registration APIs</a> and <a href={source + 'protocol/specs/observe-transport-v1.md'}>engine examples and subscription APIs</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="trace">Inspect test runs with Gua.Testing Trace</h2>
  <p><code>Gua.Testing</code> provides Trace recording/read APIs and embeds an offline Viewer. Use it to inspect operations, correlated host completion, observations, assertion outcomes, and explicit attachments. Inspector's <strong>Open Gua Trace (offline)</strong> opens the same data. A saved Trace can be turned into a self-contained HTML report without installing Node or Bun:</p>
  <CodeBlock title="Create a report from a saved Trace directory" lang="csharp" code={'using Gua.Testing;\n\nvar report = GuaTraceReport.WriteHtml("artifacts/trace/run-1", "artifacts/report.html");\nif (!report.Succeeded) Console.WriteLine(report.Error);'} />
  <p>Use a new output path. Trace records observed facts and missing data; it does not replay a journey, infer that a timeout rolled back input, or change the test result. Configure sensitive-data handling before capture; semantic baseline masks do not redact separate Trace observations or PNGs. See <a href={source + 'protocol/specs/trace-v1.md'}>Trace recording APIs</a> and <a href={source + 'protocol/specs/trace-viewer.md'}>Viewer/report usage</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="locators">Wait for actionable UI with Gua.Testing</h2>
  <p><code>GuaAssertions.Query(context)</code> creates locators that resolve a current strict single match when used. Locator actions wait for visibility, enabled state, and the advertised action, then wait for correlated host completion. Multiple matches fail immediately. Use a separate state wait for the screen change you expect after the action.</p>
  <CodeBlock title="Click, then wait for the game screen" lang="csharp" code={'using Gua.Testing;\n\nvar play = GuaAssertions.Query(context).ByRole("button", "Play");\nawait play.ClickAsync(timeout: TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));\nawait GuaAssertions.Query(context).ById("game-screen").ResolveAsync();'} />
  <p>Resolution, enqueue, and completion share one timeout budget. Rejected enqueue and host failure are not automatically retried; cancellation after sending does not guarantee rollback. See the <a href={source + 'bindings/dotnet/src/Gua.Testing/README.md'}>locator API</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="lint">Check semantic structure with Gua.Core</h2>
  <p><code>Gua.Core.GuaSemanticLinter.Analyze</code> analyzes a local context or already projected saved UI/World snapshots. Use it to find duplicate IDs, broken parent/UI references, missing accessible names, and role/action/state contradictions. The bridge's explicit <code>semantic_lint</code> command checks the host's projected trees. Choose your own failure threshold; lint does not modify the trees. See the <a href={source + 'bindings/dotnet/src/Gua.Core/GuaSemanticLint.cs'}>Core API</a> and <a href={source + 'protocol/specs/semantic-lint-v1.md'}>lint rules</a>.</p>
  <p>For writing tests interactively, Inspector generates C# locators, actions, and assertions for the selected node using <code>Gua.Testing</code>. Review the generated code against your context and ID lifetime before adding it to a test. See <a href="/inspector/">Inspector setup</a> and the <a href={source + 'packages/inspector/src/codegen.ts'}>code generator</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="spatial">Query physics through Gua.Runtime and Gua.Testing</h2>
  <p>A game-side adapter binds and authorizes a Testing/Debug spatial provider through <code>Gua.Runtime</code>. Local callers use <code>GuaRuntimeSpatialClient</code>; external tests use <code>Gua.Testing.GuaWebSocketContext.GetSpatialInfo</code>/<code>QuerySpatialBatch</code>. <code>gui-mcp</code> exposes <code>get_spatial_info</code>/<code>query_spatial_batch</code> against that provider. A Debug observation profile alone does not enable physics queries.</p>
  <p>Queries are bounded and invalidated by reset, scene/provider changes, or revoked access. Godot 4.7 GodotPhysics3D/Jolt and Unity 6000.5.3f1 desktop Mono/PhysX are the tested engine combinations. Privileged spatial queries through built-in <code>gua-webmcp</code> browser ports are unsupported. World radius searches are separate semantic-tree operations. See <a href={source + 'bindings/dotnet/src/Gua.Testing/GuaWebSocketContext.Spatial.cs'}>remote query APIs</a> and <a href={source + 'docs/spatial-engine-r1.md'}>provider setup</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="input">Discover input values and replay with Gua.Testing.Recording</h2>
  <p>Game-side registrations through <code>Gua.Runtime</code>, the Godot addon, or Unity UPM can publish bounded <code>valueSchema</code> and design-time <code>examples</code>. Capable <code>gui-mcp</code>/<code>gua-webmcp</code> clients explicitly use <code>get_game_input_actions_v2</code>/<code>find_game_input_actions_v2</code> when <code>semantic_game_input_metadata_v1</code> is present. Examples describe accepted values; host permissions and confirmation requirements still control execution. See <a href="/game-input/">game-input setup</a> and the <a href={source + 'protocol/specs/input-action-metadata-v1.md'}>metadata APIs</a>.</p>
  <p><code>Gua.Testing.Recording</code> provides ordinary sequential <code>GuaReplayer.ReplayAsync</code> and explicit <code>ReplayTimedSegmentAsync</code> for finite game-input plans. Timed replay uses an <code>IGuaTimedSegmentHost</code>; the supplied <code>Gua.Testing.Recording.GuaRuntimeSegmentHost</code> is an in-process realtime FIFO host. Set offsets, execution/cleanup budgets, and explicit releases. Mixed UI/input plans and condition waits do not belong inside a Timed Segment. Built-in MCP/Inspector replay remains sequential; it does not provide timed execution. See the <a href={source + 'bindings/dotnet/src/Gua.Testing.Recording/README.md'}>Recording installation and Timed Segment example</a>.</p>
  <h2 id="distribution">Install and pin 1.1.0</h2>
  <p>The npm and NuGet packages are available at 1.1.0. Use <code>--version 1.1.0</code> for .NET and <code>@1.1.0</code> for npm, and keep the game runtime and test packages aligned. <code>Gua.Testing</code> also supplies embedded schemas through <code>GuaDistribution.ReadSchema</code>/<code>ValidateJson</code> and the offline Viewer; use those packaged resources for offline consumers. See <a href="/docs/dotnet-packages/">NuGet packages</a>, <a href="/docs/value/">Value installation</a>, and <a href={source + 'docs/distribution-contract.md'}>resource APIs</a>.</p>
  <Callout kind="warn" title="Engine archives"><p>The <a href="https://github.com/gua-project/gua/releases/tag/gua-v1.1.0">1.1.0 GitHub Release</a> currently has no Godot addon, Unity UPM, native archive, or Inspector installer assets. To use those integrations at 1.1.0, build from <code>gua-v1.1.0</code> using the <a href={source + 'README.md'}>source build instructions</a>. Archive-download workflows need a release with the required assets and matching package versions.</p></Callout>
  <h2 id="support">Support limits when choosing an engine</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Current status</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Unity 6000.5.3f1 desktop Mono Players: Windows x64, Linux x64, Intel macOS, Apple Silicon macOS</td><td>Implemented and verified.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unity Editor Play Mode: Windows, Linux, native Apple Silicon macOS (Apple M4)</td><td>Implemented and verified with Unity 6000.5.3f1.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Unity Editor Play Mode: native Intel macOS</td><td>Implemented but unverified.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Built-in browser WebMCP privileged spatial queries</td><td>Unsupported.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Simultaneous Codex native MCP and browser WebMCP routing</td><td>Unverified.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>Intel Mono Player support is distinct from native Intel Editor verification. Desktop spatial support does not extend to WebGL/IL2CPP spatial queries. See the <a href="/docs/unity-reference/">Unity reference</a> for engine/API limits. Network mocks and deterministic starting state belong in the game and test fixture.</p>
</DocPage>
